@page "/build-log"

<PageTitle>Build log · Denny Kim</PageTitle>

<div class="pf-hero">
    <h1>Build log</h1>
    <p>The running story of how this portfolio — and the sandbox behind it — came to be. Newest first.</p>
</div>

<div class="pf-section">
    <div class="pf-timeline">
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 8, 2026 · 12:50 AM</div>
            <h3>Links fixed for the /portfolio/ base path</h3>
            <p>GitHub Pages serves the site under <code>/portfolio/</code>, but the blueprint cards, breadcrumbs, and prev/next pager used root-relative links (<code>/blueprints/home</code>) — clicking one dropped the <code>/portfolio/</code> segment and landed on GitHub's 404. Every app link is now base-relative. Lesson learned: never <code>href="/…"</code> on this site.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 11:40 PM</div>
            <h3>The portfolio goes live on GitHub Pages</h3>
            <p>Converted the site from Blazor Server to Blazor WebAssembly so it hosts as free static files. Demo images and the icon resolver became inline SVG data URIs (no server endpoints); a GitHub Actions workflow publishes and deploys on every push to <code>main</code>. Live at <code>dennykimosabi.github.io/portfolio</code> — annotate on the live site, paste the block, push, and it's live in minutes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 10:25 PM</div>
            <h3>Comment composer becomes a floating card</h3>
            <p>Restyled the <code>SiteFeedback</code> composer from a reference screenshot: a floating white card anchored at the click point, with the selected-element caption, a textarea, and a toolbar (add, camera, text-size, blue send). The send button copies the <code>[site-feedback]</code> block to the clipboard.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 10:05 PM</div>
            <h3>Annotate button always visible</h3>
            <p>Removed the <code>?feedback=1</code> gate — the Annotate button now shows on every page, no query string needed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 10:00 PM</div>
            <h3>Product Gallery: three explorable versions</h3>
            <p>Reworked the workshop's Product Gallery section into three side-by-side explorations: Thumbnails (the existing gallery), Grid (swipe carousel on mobile, three-column grid with a full-width lead image on desktop), and Lightbox (carousel with dots plus a fullscreen viewer with arrows and image count). New components <code>ProductGalleryGrid</code> and <code>ProductGalleryLightbox</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 9:45 PM</div>
            <h3>Claude Design research</h3>
            <p>Researched Anthropic's Claude Design — chat panel plus live canvas, inline comments on selected elements, direct text editing, AI-generated sliders — to mirror its rebuildable editing features. Gaps noted: no version history, no Figma export. A canvas edit view is on the roadmap.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 9:30 PM</div>
            <h3>Blueprint detail pages get their chrome</h3>
            <p>Added <code>BlueprintFrame</code> around the five blueprint pages, modeled on Blueprint's own blueprint detail pages: breadcrumb back to the gallery, title and description, component chips, and a prev/next pager across all five blueprints.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026 · 9:00 PM</div>
            <h3>/blueprints gallery</h3>
            <p>New gallery snapshotting five storefront pages — Home, Search, Product, Cart, Accessories — as live scaled-down previews with component counts, each linking to its full page. Added to the nav and home page.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Design tokens go global</h3>
            <p>Finished the token cleanup Blueprint-style: zero hardcoded values left in the library. A <code>--sf-font-sans</code> token replaced 20 hardcoded font stacks; 35 remaining hardcoded colors were folded into tokens (near-grays consolidated, guided-navigation alert colors mapped onto the feedback families); new tokens for faint text, visited links, tint borders, and drawer/popover shadows; the dead dark-theme block deleted. Added a <strong>Design Tokens</strong> gallery to the workshop — live swatches for every token.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Button docs match Blueprint; comment tool restyled</h3>
            <p>Restructured the <code>SfButton</code> workshop story into Blueprint-style labeled subsections (Variants, Sizes, With icons, Icon-only, States, As link) with one-line parameter descriptions. Restyled the <code>SiteFeedback</code> tool itself to match — primary blue instead of purple, light banner, Remix chat icon.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Feedback fixes round 1</h3>
            <p>First edits through the new point-and-comment tool: <code>SfSelect</code> lost its native browser arrow for a Remix chevron with proper spacing; RefineSearch accessory tiles got their hover state back (image zoom on the overlay layout); filter chips went full-width with the close button right-aligned as a Remix icon.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>SfButton reaches Blueprint parity</h3>
            <p>Extended <code>SfButton</code> to match the <a href="https://blazorblueprintui.com/docs/components/button">Blueprint button spec</a>: icon-only square sizes, <code>Icon</code> + <code>IconPosition</code> fragments, <code>LoadingText</code>, <code>Href</code> rendering an anchor, and a typed <code>SfButtonType</code> enum. Added <code>SfButtonGroup</code> with separators, text labels, and horizontal/vertical orientation. Restructured the button docs into labeled subsections like Blueprint's page.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Point-and-comment feedback tool</h3>
            <p>Built <code>SiteFeedback</code>: click Annotate on any page, click any element, type a note, and copy a <code>[site-feedback]</code> block with the page, element label, and CSS selector. It turned site review into a tight loop — precise edit notes with selectors instead of prose descriptions.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>SearchToolbar rebuilt on the atomic layer</h3>
            <p>Rebuilt the search toolbar's bottom row with the new primitives: filter toggle as an outline button, page-size as a button group, sort as <code>SfSelect</code>, view toggle as an icon button group — all Remix icons. Deleted the hand-rolled toggle/select CSS it replaced.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Component API cleanup</h3>
            <p>All 38 components now accept <code>Class</code> and <code>AdditionalAttributes</code> — Blueprint-style escape hatches — and all ~200 parameters carry XML documentation comments, which feed the auto-generated props tables in the workshop.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Workshop becomes Blueprint-style docs</h3>
            <p>Restyled the component gallery after Blueprint's docs: Preview/Code tabs per component, a copy button, live previews, and reflection-generated props tables. Pop-out clipping fixed so drawers and dropdowns escape preview containers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>BlazingStory trial</h3>
            <p>Scaffolded a local stories project linking the real components — builds clean, serves on <code>localhost:5293</code>, and its MCP endpoint exposes the component API to Claude Code. Still local and unpushed; the workshop stays the human-facing docs.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Atomic component layer</h3>
            <p>Built the primitives everything else composes from: <code>SfButton</code>, <code>SfBadge</code>, <code>SfChip</code>, <code>SfInput</code>, <code>SfSelect</code>, <code>SfIcon</code> (Remix Icon, now the default set), <code>SfLink</code>, <code>SfQuantityStepper</code>, <code>SfDivider</code>, <code>SfSkeleton</code>, <code>SfTooltip</code>. Twenty button-containing components were refactored onto <code>SfButton</code>/<code>SfChip</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>Light mode only, .NET 9, interactivity fix</h3>
            <p>Three foundation calls in one day: the whole site converted to Blueprint light/zinc (no dark mode, by decision); the project retargeted from <code>net10.0</code> back to <code>net9.0</code>; and <code>Routes</code>/<code>HeadOutlet</code> got the <code>InteractiveServer</code> render mode, which restored the cart drawer, quantities, dropdowns, and fitment presets.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 7, 2026</div>
            <h3>This portfolio becomes a design component catalog</h3>
            <p>Rebuilt the portfolio itself as an app-like Blazor site around the 38 existing storefront components — workshop, build log, experiments, archive. The portfolio is the proof, not just the frame. Inspired by Patrick Morgan's Design OS at Sublime Security.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">October 2026</div>
            <h3>Storefront.Preview: the localhost test that confirmed everything</h3>
            <p>A preview harness compiling the real client components against in-memory dummy data — <code>dotnet run</code>, open localhost, click through. No database, no backend, no waiting. This was the moment the sandbox approach proved itself as a design tool, not just an engineering toy.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">September – October 2026</div>
            <h3>Storefront.Sandbox: 38 components, full pages</h3>
            <p>Built out the complete component library with Claude Code — vehicle picker, refine search, assembly results, product cards, price boxes, cart, reviews — plus full storefront pages (home, search, product, cart, accessories) and a BlazingStory workshop.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">September 2026</div>
            <h3>Design-to-development ecosystem mapping</h3>
            <p>Documented the whole pipeline: token architecture, the Claude Code runbook with prompts, Figma route evaluation, and the business case for AI-assisted frontend work. The process became as much a deliverable as the components.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">2026</div>
            <h3>The handoff problem</h3>
            <p>SimplePart modernizes to .NET + Blazor; design moves from Sketch to Figma. Static mockups keep losing fidelity in translation. The question: what if design handed developers working prototypes instead?</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-tl-item">
            <div class="pf-tl-date">Before</div>
            <h3>Nine years of e-commerce UX at SimplePart</h3>
            <p>OEM parts storefronts for dealers — fitment-first information architecture, assembly diagrams, supersession chains, dealer pricing stacks. The domain knowledge everything else builds on.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
